inputsynth: absolute pointer moves, W3C Meta = Super, interruptible pauses,
a persistent --stdin mode, and flushed zero-duration mouse moves

1. Upstream adds every mouse move to the previous *target* and stores the raw
   move value, so the 2nd+ move of one action chain lands in the wrong place.
   Krema E2E needs multi-step hover paths and press-move-release drags inside
   one chain, so apply W3C semantics: "viewport" = absolute, "pointer" =
   relative to the previous position.
2. W3C "Meta" (U+E03D) is the Windows/Command key. Upstream maps it to the
   Meta_L keysym, which in the evdev keymap lives on <META> (keycode 205) at
   shift level, so "Meta+F5" is sent as Shift+<META>+F5 and never matches a
   Meta+F5 KGlobalAccel shortcut. Map it to Super_L (<LWIN>, KEY_LEFTMETA),
   which is what KDE calls Meta.
3. A pause action with "interruptible": true ends early on request: the
   request ends the interruptible pause in progress, or the next one (other
   pauses ignore it). A mid-drag check holds the button in a pause of the
   drag's own chain while it inspects the screen; the test releases it as
   soon as it is done instead of always sitting out a worst-case fixed pause.
   With an action file the request is SIGUSR1.
4. `inputsynth --stdin` stays up and reads one request per line: {"id": n,
   "actions": [...]} runs a chain and is answered with {"id": n, "ok": true}
   on stdout when it has finished; {"resume": n} is the request of 3. for
   chain n only (a finished chain ignores it, so it never reaches a later
   chain). Starting a process, connecting to Wayland and registering a
   fake-input device per chain cost ~100 ms; the E2E suite sends ~500 chains
   per run through one process and one device. Each chain runs on a thread
   of its own, so a request arriving while an earlier chain sits in a pause
   (e.g. Escape during a drag) is not held up. Consequences: the roundtrip
   after each request waits on a per-thread event queue when called off the
   GUI thread (Qt dispatches the default queue there), and the pointer
   state shared by all chains is guarded by a mutex.
5. A zero-duration mouse move no longer waits for the server after its
   point: the request is enqueued and flushed right away and marked pending
   on the chain's thread. The suite's hover paths send tens of these per
   chain, each previously paying a roundtrip. If a flush cannot write
   everything (EAGAIN) the move falls back to the roundtrip it used to do,
   so buffered requests never sit out the pauses that follow them. The
   chain is answered only after any motion still pending on its thread has
   been confirmed by a roundtrip, so a pure-motion chain costs one
   roundtrip instead of one per point. A later action's own barrier
   (button press/release, key, touch, wheel, an interpolated move's step)
   already confirms it, so there is no extra roundtrip then. The flag is
   per thread: another chain's barrier must not clear it, its sync
   callback may return while our motion is not even on the wire yet.
   Positive-duration mouse moves keep their final roundtrip and
   interpolated steps unchanged so the compositor sees every intermediate
   position in real time, and touch moves keep theirs so every motion
   still carries its own frame.

